  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1147 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nTemperatures are cool tonight with broad high pressure centered\nover the Plains driving our weather. The current freeze\nwarning/frost advisory split still looks reasonable for tonight\nand will not make any changes. It is a little warmer over\nsoutheast Missouri right now where 4-5kft stratus persists but\nthe trend is towards clearing and the odds seem to support\ngetting cleared out.\n\nTonight the ridge axis will set mostly on the area with strong\nsubsidence aloft. All guidance also brings in very dry \nsfc-850mb or so air and I won't be surprised to see dewpoints \nmix down this afternoon and a very supportive environment for \nheavy dew/frost overnight tonight. NBM overnight lows are \nconsensus 31-33 degrees which is a little lower than some of the\nraw model output but I think it has the right idea given how \nmuch subsidence/dry air and clearing is approaching. For \nmessaging simplicity just converted the freeze watch to a freeze\nwarning for all 58-counties.\n\n00z deterministic GFS is a little more aggressive with moisture\nreturn and the right rear quadrant of a jet max on Wednesday\nbringing some rain/storm chances. Pretty much everything else,\nincluding GFS ensemble members are less aggressive with this,\nwill be worth watching the trends for the specifics as the GFS\nsolution yields at least a little bit of instability. Both\ndeterministic GFS/ECMWF and available ensemble members then drag\na large deep layer cyclone over the Great Lakes region that\nsends cooler air this direction. The source airmass is not\nespecially frigid but it looks to put our highs into the 50s by\nthe end of the upcoming week.",
